; La Aventura Original, part one: dying, and what has to happen next.
;
; Five commands south from the forest walk off the cliff at 28, and the fall
; is the one path in the adventure that ends a game without winning it. It is
; worth a script of its own because it is the place a port gets wrong without
; anybody noticing: the adventure's own tables say nothing about ending, so
; what the player sees afterwards depends entirely on what the port put there.
;
; In 1989 the interpreter printed the turn report and asked for another game.
; Under DAAD v3 that is the END condact's job, and a death screen without one
; leaves the player standing in location 29 answering "No puedes ir al Sur."
; for ever — which is what this script would catch. See 11.4 of
; docs/DAAD_v2_v3_Research/07-daad-v3.md for why the original has no END of
; its own and why the port has to supply it.
;
;   qdaad compile -i examples/AO/ZXSpectrum/+3/part1/game.sce -m pc -s vga -f ddb -o tmp/AO1
;   qrun -i tmp/AO1.ddb -T examples/AO/test/part1-death.qtest

TEST "La Aventura Original, parte I: la muerte en el abismo"

SEED 1989

STEP "Pantalla de título y bosque"
  EXPECT "?"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "AVENTURA ORIGINAL"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "LA RESPUESTA DEPENDE DE TU VALOR"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "Estás en el bosque"
  ASSERT AT = 3
END

; Down the valley to the precipice. The adventure lets you walk off it: the
; four locations in between say nothing about the drop, and the fifth order is
; the last one of this game.
STEP "Al sur hasta el precipicio"
  INPUT  S
  ASSERT AT = 24

  INPUT  S
  ASSERT AT = 25

  INPUT  S
  ASSERT AT = 26

  INPUT  S
  EXPECT "precipicio rocoso"
  ASSERT AT = 28
END

STEP "El salto, y el informe de la partida"
  INPUT  S
  EXPECT "emprendes el vuelo"
  EXPECT "en el fondo del abismo"
  EXPECT "un poco muerto"

  ; The report is table 21, which is the port's stand-in for the one the 1989
  ; interpreter printed. Five orders is the five S above, so the counter has to
  ; agree with the script: flag 31 counting wrong is a bug the plural hides.
  EXPECT "Has dado 5 ordenes."
  ASSERT TURNS = 5
  ASSERT AT = 29
END

; And here is the whole point of the script. The game is over, so it has to ask
; and it has to listen: anything else, and the player is in limbo.
;
; A yes puts the player back at location 0, which is the title, so the new game
; asks for its three keys again exactly as the first one did. That is the
; adventure's own doing and not the interpreter's: table 3 draws the title at
; AT 0, and END zeroing the flags is what sends the player there.
STEP "Pregunta otra partida, y una respuesta reinicia"
  EXPECT "¿Hace otra partidita?"
  INPUT  S

  EXPECT "?"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "AVENTURA ORIGINAL"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "LA RESPUESTA DEPENDE DE TU VALOR"
  KEY    SPACE

  EXPECT "Estás en el bosque"
  ASSERT AT = 3
  ASSERT TURNS = 0
END

STEP "Y en la partida nueva se puede volver a morir"
  INPUT  S
  INPUT  S
  INPUT  S
  INPUT  S
  INPUT  S
  EXPECT "un poco muerto"
  EXPECT "Has dado 5 ordenes."
  ASSERT AT = 29
END

; Saying no is the other answer, and it is the one that has to stop. A machine
; of the period dropped the player back to BASIC or to the system prompt; here
; it is the end of the run, which is what qrun has instead of a prompt.
STEP "Y decir que no termina"
  EXPECT "¿Hace otra partidita?"
  INPUT  N
  ASSERT ENDED
END
